{"kind":"report","text":"Narrow delta review of notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md at exact head ad3dfd7a086ca9019348fe107a552929092414a8 (worktree and origin both there, tree clean), closing my ratified third review f3464534. Earlier verdicts on this design: f9d9f9b7 and 802980ff, both changes-requested and both ratified.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED. The deltas are clean. All five findings L1-L5 are closed against the note's own text, the agent-opponent addition is sound on every axis the request names, and the diff contains nothing beyond the two described commits. Said plainly, as asked: this is the round that closes it, and I have no design objection left.\n\nOne thing hugh must read before merging, and it is not a design objection: the named gate is RED at this exact head, for a reason external to the head. Details in section D — the short version is that the branch predates a doc re-anchoring that landed on main while this review was in flight, main is green, and merging fixes it.\n\n=== A. THE FIVE FINDINGS, BY NAME ===\n\nL1 (the causal chain dropped from the vocabulary) — CLOSED, and closed better than I asked. The vocabulary now reads: \"`join` (points at the create it answers; carries the join secret when the game demands one; the first join the fold accepts seats the opponent), `move` (each move points at the one before it, and the first move points at the join — the causal chain is carried in the signed record itself, which is what lets the fold detect a wrong turn or a second join from the log's own structure)\". Two things make this a real fix rather than a restored phrase. It says the chain is \"carried in the signed record itself\", which is precisely the distinction that went missing — a causal reference in the signed intent, not a payload field that would sit outside the provenance graph. And it states the consequence, that the fold detects a wrong turn or a second join from log structure, so an implementer knows what the chain is for rather than only that it exists. It does all that in plain words, so nothing was traded against the rewrite's purpose.\n\nL2 (self-hoster budget miscounted) — CLOSED. \"Clone (or fork) the chess repository, build, `chess init`, `chess serve`, play on loopback. Budget: three commands after clone.\" Three listed, three counted.\n\nL3 (the tier-2 requirement count misdescribed its source) — CLOSED. Now \"adopts — or explicitly amends — three of that note's tier-2 requirements rather than inheriting them by reference\". A new reader no longer infers that the deployment note contains only three.\n\nL4 (play-first-anchor-later thinned to an inference) — CLOSED. Restored as a stated decision: \"It lands either as evidence on the join or as a free-standing statement afterwards, and the after-the-fact path is the normal one — play first, anchor later is the ordering the whole adoption story demands, and the vocabulary is built for it.\" Both landing sites and the normative ordering are back.\n\nL5 (storage topology not declared) — CLOSED. \"The repository of record lives on a forge (GitHub or similar) — the *forge-primary* storage shape, and this design declares that choice rather than leaving it implied.\" That is the declaration the deployment note requires, made explicitly.\n\n=== B. THE AGENT-OPPONENT ADDITION ===\n\nJudged at the standing severity. It is sound, and it is the cheapest possible way to add agents: no new event kinds, no new identity concepts, one new surface, and the existing vocabulary reused verbatim.\n\nTruth discipline — PRESERVED. \"The agent brings its own chess judgment; the fold judges legality identically for every player, and an agent's illegal move is refused the same way a human's is.\" That is the append-then-judge rule applied uniformly, with no privileged path for a non-browser client. I re-read the truth-discipline paragraph at this head to be sure the addition did not quietly except itself, and it is unchanged and still governs: the board renders only what the fold has judged, and a refused act is surfaced rather than swallowed.\n\nThe one-rules-engine rule — PRESERVED, and the note draws the distinction correctly. \"There is exactly one implementation of chess in the system\" is intact and unchanged at this head. The addition does not contradict it, because \"the agent brings its own chess judgment\" is move *selection*, which is what any player does, not legality *determination*, which is what the rule is about. The mechanism that keeps it true is in the MCP surface list itself: \"query legal destinations\" is exposed to the agent, answered by the fold's own engine, exactly as it is to the browser. So an agent never needs to implement the rules, and if it plays something illegal anyway the fold refuses it identically. Worth stating for the record, since it is the interesting case: an LLM agent will in practice carry an approximate, unreliable model of chess rules in its head. That is outside the system boundary and is simply what having a fallible player means. The design is right that the answer is not to trust it and not to forbid it, but to let the fold judge.\n\nIdentity model — PRESERVED. The strength-inheritance rule is intact and unchanged at this head: \"An agent credential is exactly as strong as the anchor that minted it, on both axes, and the interface says which.\" The new bullet cross-references Identity rather than restating it, and adds a property that is true and load-bearing: \"If the agent's key was minted under a person's anchor, the log also shows whose agent it is; either way its moves are its own signatures, distinguishable from its owner's forever.\" That is the non-repudiation half of the dual-signature chain, and \"either way\" correctly covers the unanchored agent, which is a legitimate player under the same rule that makes an anonymous human one. The walkthrough agrees: \"the agent joins with its own signing key and plays its own moves... every move it makes is signed with its key, so the history always shows exactly who (or what) played what.\"\n\nLayer-contract fit — CORRECT. An MCP adapter is a layer-6 surface, and the note's parallel (\"exactly as gitseq's own service does for its workroom\") matches how architecture.md describes `cmd/gitseq-mcp`: a surface that adapts MCP calls to application operations, where \"the MCP protocol is a surface contract, not the Workroom fold\". The compatibility table already treats surface as its own axis. The adapter exposes only fold-derived reads (games list, board, legal destinations) and signed durable acts (move, resign), so it infers no application force the interpreter did not produce, which is the bar layer 6 sets.\n\nFriction budget — HONEST, and it is now the best-constructed budget in the document. \"For an agent that already has its key and MCP access to the service: paste the invitation link. Budget: one step. First-time setup is the agent-credential path above, plus pointing the agent's MCP configuration at the deployment — counted when walked, and the walk must include an agent that has never seen the service before.\" It does the three things I have been asking for across three rounds in one bullet: it names the population the number applies to, it refuses to hide the cold-start cost and itemizes both of its parts, and its last clause is an anti-cheat that stops the acceptance measurement from only ever exercising the warm path. The \"one step\" is measured incrementally over an established session, which is the same convention the anchored-player paths use, so it is consistent rather than flattering.\n\nWork items 4 and 8 are updated consistently with the addition — item 4 gains \"the MCP adapter exposing the same acts so agents can play\" (\"the same acts\" being the right commitment: one surface, no second vocabulary), and item 8 gains an agent-played seat in acceptance.\n\nThree small non-blocking notes for the implementing heads, none of which changes a decision:\n\n1. Key custody on the MCP path is not stated. \"The chess binary also exposes its acts over an MCP adapter\" together with \"pointing the agent's MCP configuration at the deployment\" can be read as a server-side endpoint, and the note does not say where the agent's signature is produced. The rest of the document settles it — the agent holds its own key, the walkthrough says it joins with its own signing key, and no deployment ever holds an actor's private key — so this is a clause to add in work item 4, not an ambiguity in the model.\n2. The agent budget requires that the walk include \"an agent that has never seen the service before\", but acceptance item 8 asks only for \"one seat played by an agent over the MCP adapter\". Align item 8 with the budget's requirement, or the anti-cheat clause depends on a reviewer remembering it.\n3. Two lines introduced by this head run past the document's wrap width (the restored L4 sentence, and the short line left in the forge-primary hunk). Cosmetic, but this note is about to be read by newcomers.\n\nCarried forward unchanged from earlier rounds, still non-blocking: the agent-credential path's \"three manual steps\" does not count the endorsement prompt or the agent runtime install, though this head improves matters by counting the MCP configuration step; the dual-signature requirement on `anchor` is still implied rather than stated; motion-hint authorship is still unstated, so a watcher could animate a piece on other watchers' boards; chat abuse on a public live channel is unaddressed while volume abuse on submit is; and the invitation still carries two mechanisms where only the join-secret variant can serve the anonymous path.\n\n=== C. DIFF SCOPE ===\n\nConfirmed clean. `git diff 11633f05 ad3dfd7a` is one file, 46 insertions and 12 deletions, in eight hunks, and every hunk maps to a stated delta: the walkthrough alternate ending; the vocabulary causal chain (L1); the agents-are-players bullet; the tier-2 requirement count (L3); forge-primary declared (L5); play-first-anchor-later restored (L4); the agent budget plus the self-hoster count (L2); and work items 4 and 8 updated as consequences of the agent addition. Nothing else changed anywhere in the tree — no docs, no code, no other notes. There is no unstated change requiring full-severity review.\n\n=== D. THE GATE, REPORTED HONESTLY ===\n\ngo test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... at exact head ad3dfd7a, clean worktree: FAIL, 87.418s, exit 1. The failures are citation-anchor failures in three pages — docs/reference/event-identifiers.md, docs/reference/gs/provenance.md, docs/reference/gs/review.md — reported as \"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e37ac7f4c061410d50e88f2af22bc03da84e7f82 is retired, so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer\", plus \"24 citations remain known-failing; see citation_baseline.txt\".\n\nThis is not caused by the head, and I established that rather than assuming it. Three checks. First, `git diff 11633f05 ad3dfd7a -- docs/` is empty: these two commits touch no documentation at all, only the note. Second, the same gate at current main (5cabbb74) passes: ok, 88.109s, exit 0. Third, `git diff main ad3dfd7a -- docs/` lists nineteen files, including all three that fail, so the branch is simply behind main — it still carries the pre-re-anchoring copies of pages whose citations were repaired on main by the merges that landed while this review was in flight. A retirement propagates through the durable log, which every worktree shares, so a branch cut before the repair fails the gate even though its own commits are innocent. That is the flare mechanism working as designed, pointed at a stale branch rather than at this change.\n\nWhy this does not block the approval, and what hugh should do with it. The branch adds exactly one file under notes/, and the docset gate reads docs/ only — as I have now noted in all four of these reviews. Merging ad3dfd7a into main therefore produces a tree with main's already-repaired docs plus this note, and the gate is green on that tree. I state that as reasoning from the three measurements above, not as a run I performed; I did not merge and will not. The alternative — refreshing the branch from main first — would change the head and by the AGENTS.md rule would invalidate this very approval, so it is the worse path unless hugh wants it for another reason. What I would not do is let the request's sentence \"the docset gate passes at this exact head\" stand unqualified in the record: it was true when the planner ran it and is false now, for reasons that have nothing to do with the design.\n\n=== E. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. This head adds one layer-6 surface commitment — the chess binary's MCP adapter — and changes no contract. The adapter mirrors `cmd/gitseq-mcp`'s established role and exposes only fold-derived reads and signed acts, so the layer-6 rule against surfaces inferring unproduced force holds. Everything I checked in earlier rounds is unchanged at this head: kernel untouched, the live layer used as the contract defines, read-binding then select then fold, bounded projections, two labeled cursors. The design's own contract change still travels in work item 1 with its architecture-reference row in the same implementing head. No architecture objection.\n\nSECURITY. Nothing reopened. The addition's security content is a gain rather than a cost: an agent's acts are its own signatures and stay distinguishable from its owner's forever, which is exactly the property that makes delegated agent play auditable, and it follows from the kernel proving who signed rather than from anything new. The strength-inheritance rule and the two-axis display are intact. The one item to nail down in implementation is where the agent's signature is produced on the MCP path (note 1 above); the governing invariant that no deployment holds an actor's private key is stated elsewhere in the same document and is not weakened here.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The agent addition is close to free: no new event kinds, no new identity vocabulary, no second rules engine, and the MCP adapter exposes \"the same acts\" rather than a parallel surface. The restored causal chain is likewise a net gain in clarity over what it replaced, because it now explains what the chain is for. I found nothing in these two commits worth cutting.\n\n=== F. CLOSING ===\nFour rounds, and the design closes with every finding I raised resolved in the text rather than in a summary: the fold-profile-hash impossibility, the missing invitation, the anchor statement and seat recovery, the misaimed one-encoder rule, the collapsed anchor ladder, fold determinism under expiry, the writer lease and orphaned acknowledgements, the deployer secrets, the Firefox prompt, the unamended capability chain, and the causal chain. The friction budgets are now honest on all eight paths. I approve this head, and the only thing standing between it and a clean merge is a stale branch that the merge itself resolves.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c13cf8a4a6a3f697033f13c531d34b0310599a38","head":"ad3dfd7a086ca9019348fe107a552929092414a8","verdict":"approved"}}